   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Now line by line, with the meaning.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Может быть, стоило проснуться раньше?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Maybe we should have woken up sooner?' Russian says stoilo, literally 'it was worth it', for 'should have', and ranshe means 'earlier'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Проснуться раньше?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Wake up sooner?' In Russian prosnutsya is waking up yourself, while razbudit means 'to wake someone else'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Может быть.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Maybe.' Russian mozhet byt, literally 'it can be', is the everyday 'maybe'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "А может, и нет.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Or maybe not.' The little word i before net adds 'even', and mozhet alone is a shorter 'maybe'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Никто не знает.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Nobody knows.' Russian needs both nikto, 'nobody', and ne, 'not', rather than just one negative. And the last words again.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Может быть, стоило проснуться раньше?",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Проснуться раньше?",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Может быть.",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "А может, и нет.",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Никто не знает.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "The season ends on an open question. The verb prosnutsya, 'to wake up', is one you'll need every morning in Russian. See you next time!",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "До встречи!",
   "gap": 0.3
